---
layout: article
title: Audit logs
description: Appwrite provides audit logs to help detect anomalies and investigate security incidents.
---

All Appwrite products, like Authentication, Databases, Storage, Functions, and Messaging, provide detailed audit logs.
Audit logs are important in detecting and responding to security incidents.
Through audit logs, you can detect incidents through anomalous activities,
trace the source of security incidents, and understand the scope of users affected so you can respond more quickly
and effectively.

# Access audit logs {% #access-audit-logs %}

You can access audit logs for different products under the **Activity** tab where applicable.
Logs are available for tables, rows, and individual users.

{% only_dark %}
![Project Activity tab with reasons](/images/docs/advanced/security/dark/activity.avif)
{% /only_dark %}

{% only_light %}
![Project Activity tab with reasons](/images/docs/advanced/security/activity.avif)
{% /only_light %}

# Format {% #format %}

Audit log entries under the **Activity** tab have the following structure.
Each entry describes an event.

{% table %}
- Column
- Description
---
- Event
- The name of the [event](/docs/apis/events).
---
- Actor
- The user, API key, or other actor that performed the event.
---
- Type
- The kind of actor, such as a user or an API key. Events sent through the [MCP server](/docs/tooling/ai/mcp-servers) or the [CLI](/docs/tooling/command-line/installation) are also marked **Via MCP** or **Via CLI**.
---
- Resource
- The resource the event changed, such as a table, row, or function.
---
- Reason
- Why the request was made, when the client sent one. See [Record a reason](#reason).
---
- IP address
- The IP address the request came from.
---
- Country
- The country the request came from, based on its IP address.
---
- Time
- The date and time of the event.
{% /table %}

Select an event to open its details, including the full reason, the actor, and the device and location the request came from.

# Record a reason {% #reason %}

{% only_dark %}
![Activity event details with a reason](/images/docs/advanced/security/dark/activity-reason.avif)
{% /only_dark %}

{% only_light %}
![Activity event details with a reason](/images/docs/advanced/security/activity-reason.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Send the `X-Appwrite-Reason` header with an API request to record why it was made.
The reason appears in the **Reason** column of the **Activity** tab, next to the event it explains,
and in the event's details, so you can review changes made by AI agents, scripts, and teammates later.

```http
DELETE /v1/functions/checkout-legacy HTTP/1.1
X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>
X-Appwrite-Key: <API_KEY>
X-Appwrite-Reason: Moved to SSR endpoint /api/checkout in storefront site
```

A reason is one line of text of up to 256 characters. The header value is percent-decoded, so send it
encoded with `encodeURIComponent` (or your language's equivalent). That covers any language, and a literal `%` as `%25`.
Plain ASCII text without `%` can be sent as it is.

A request fails with `general_argument_invalid` before it runs when its reason is longer, isn't valid UTF-8,
or contains control characters such as line breaks, tabs or terminal escapes, or Unicode bidirectional override characters.

## From the CLI {% #reason-cli %}

Pass `--reason` to any [Appwrite CLI](/docs/tooling/command-line/commands#reason) command, or set the `APPWRITE_REASON`
environment variable for a whole shell session or CI job. The CLI encodes the reason and sends it with every request the command makes.

```sh
appwrite push functions --reason "Release 2.4: move checkout to the storefront site"
```

## From a server SDK {% #reason-sdk %}

Server SDKs don't have a dedicated method for reasons. Add the header to the client with `addHeader`, and every request the client makes carries it.
Use a separate client for each reason, so a reason doesn't stick to unrelated requests.

{% multicode %}

```server-nodejs
const sdk = require('node-appwrite');

const client = new sdk.Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setKey('<API_KEY>')
    .addHeader('X-Appwrite-Reason', encodeURIComponent('Remove test users created during the sign-up load test'));

const users = new sdk.Users(client);

await users.delete({ userId: '<USER_ID>' });
```

```server-python
from urllib.parse import quote
from appwrite.client import Client
from appwrite.services.users import Users

client = Client()
client.set_endpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
client.set_project('<PROJECT_ID>')
client.set_key('<API_KEY>')
client.add_header('X-Appwrite-Reason', quote('Remove test users created during the sign-up load test', safe=''))

users = Users(client)

users.delete(user_id = '<USER_ID>')
```

```server-php
<?php

use Appwrite\Client;
use Appwrite\Services\Users;

$client = (new Client())
    ->setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    ->setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    ->setKey('<API_KEY>')
    ->addHeader('X-Appwrite-Reason', rawurlencode('Remove test users created during the sign-up load test'));

$users = new Users($client);

$users->delete(userId: '<USER_ID>');
```

{% /multicode %}

## From the MCP server {% #reason-mcp %}

The [Appwrite MCP server](/docs/tooling/ai/mcp-servers#reasons) asks the AI agent for a reason with every change it makes
and records it on the request. Changes an agent makes show up in **Activity** with the reason the agent gave.

# Retention {% #retention %}

GDPR data retention rules require any personal data that is collected or processed to be kept
only for as long as data are required to achieve the purpose for which the information was collected.
For this reason, audit logs are retained for only 7 days for Pro organizations and 1 hour
for Free organizations.
